+Click a Python token to open its definition, or use Cmd/Ctrl-click.
 Dynamic calls and missing dependencies can remain unresolved. Existing TS/JS
 navigation continues to use TypeScript. Stale diff lines cannot be resolved against
 current workspace code.
+
+### Text-first reviews
+
+Use ordinary Markdown links such as `[TraceService](explain:trace-service)` to open a detail in a new active left-side tab. These links are validated alongside diagram and callstack references. Keep the main page to a short ordered reading path; put component definitions and implementation detail in `explain:` blocks.
+
+An explanation can include `file` (an exact workspace-relative path) and `changes` (what this diff changes in that file). The left tab shows the full explanation. The code pane has an About this file card with the exact path, file purpose and change summary directly above the patch. This card follows the displayed file, including when an overview links to several files. Select files from the list beneath the walkthrough. Explain each file represented in the patch, including tests and configuration. Describe tests by the actions and outcomes they verify, and distinguish simulated checks from live results.
+
+Diagrams are optional detail views. Keep their nodes aligned with the surrounding prose and link each unfamiliar component to its explanation. Avoid repeating the same explanation across an overview, goals, diagrams and call stacks.
+
+Detail explanations can mix prose with `callstack` (a source-annotated execution sketch), `flowCaption`, `codeExample`, `codeLanguage`, `codeCaption`, and `diagramCaption`. Diagrams render inline in Details alongside the explanation. Callstack links use the same validated explanation/source references as the main document. Label pseudocode as illustrative and state when an example record omits fields; prefer actual saved test records when demonstrating the stored format.
+
+The left pane keeps opened explanations, files and symbols in browser-style tabs. The first tab is the walkthrough overview and stays available. Opening an existing destination selects its tab. Each tab restores description and code scroll positions; close tabs with × or Delete. Arrow keys, Home and End move between tabs. Tabs last for the current page visit.
+
+### Symbol details
+
+Click a TypeScript or JavaScript symbol in a diff or definition to inspect its purpose, compiler-resolved signature, parameters, return type and members. The definition stays visible on the right, with its description on the left. Type names and members in the inspector are also selectable and use the same open-page tabs.
+
+Attach authored descriptions using an explanation’s `symbols` array, with entries such as `apps/server/TraceService.ts#TraceService` or `apps/server/RunTrace.ts#RunTrace.record`. Add `:method` or another compiler symbol kind when a static method and an instance property share the same name. The explanation’s summary, inputs, outputs and steps describe the symbol; TypeScript supplies its current contract. Without an authored explanation, the inspector uses JSDoc when available and explicitly indicates when prose is missing.
